<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout>
  <title>Practicing Whole Proofs By Contradiction</title>
  
  

  <page>
    
    <p>
        Recall a real number <m>x</m> is <term>rational</term> if
        <m>x=\frac{a}{b}</m> for some <m>a,b\in\mathbb{Z}</m> with
        <m>b\neq 0</m>.
        Otherwise, we say <m>x</m> is <term>irrational</term>.
        Recall that the set of rationals is denoted <m>\mathbb{Q}</m> (for
        quotient).
    </p>

    <p>
        You may use any of the following as lemmas without proof. We will prove <xref ref="lem-sqrt2"/> soon.
    </p>

    <lemma xml:id="lem-sqrt2">
      <statement>
        <p>
           The real number <m>\sqrt{2}</m> is irrational.
  
        </p>
      </statement>
    </lemma>

    <lemma>
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>x,y\in\mathbb{R}</m>, if <m>xy=0</m> then <m>x=0</m> or
        <m>y=0</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </lemma>

<lemma>
  <statement>
    <p>
      For all <m>a,b,x,y\in\mathbb{Z}</m> and <m>n\in\mathbb{N}</m>, if
        <m>a\equiv x \pmod{n}</m> and <m>b\equiv y\pmod{n}</m> then
        <m>ab \equiv xy\pmod{n}</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
</lemma>


  <theorem>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For all real numbers <m>a</m> 
        and <m>b</m>, if <m>a&gt;0</m> and <m>b&gt;0</m>, then <m>\frac{2}{a} + \frac{2}{b} \neq \frac{4}{a+b}</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>
  
  <proof>
    <p>
       We will use a proof by contradiction.
                    So we assume the theorem is false.
                    That is, we assume there exist real numbers <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> 
                    such that <m>\frac{2}{a} + \frac{2}{b} = \frac{4}{a+b}.</m> By algebra,
                    <md>
                      <mrow> \frac{2}{a} + \frac{2}{b}\amp = \frac{4}{a+b} </mrow>
                      <mrow> \frac{2b+2a}{ab} \amp = \frac{4}{a+b} </mrow>
                      <mrow> (2b+2a)(a+b) \amp = 4ab </mrow>
                      <mrow> 2a^2+4ab+b^2\amp = 4ab </mrow>
                      <mrow>2a^2 + 2b^2 = 0. \amp </mrow>
                    </md>
        This is a contradiction because we assumed <m>a&gt; 0</m> and <m>b &gt; 0</m>, so 
        <md>2a^2+2b^2 &gt; 0</md>.
        Thus, the negation of the theorem is false and we conclude for all real numbers <m>a</m> 
        and <m>b</m>, if <m>a&gt;0</m> and <m>b&gt;0</m>, then <m>\frac{2}{a} + \frac{2}{b} \neq \frac{4}{a+b}</m>.      
    </p>
  </proof>

</page>

  <page>
  <theorem>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For each real number <m>x</m>, <m>(\sqrt{2}+x)</m> is irrational or <m>(\sqrt{2}-x)</m> is irrational.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>
  
  <proof>
    <p>
      We will use a proof by contradiction.
                    So we assume the theorem is false.
                    That is, we assume that there exists <m>x\in\mathbb{R}</m> such that <m>(\sqrt{2}+x)</m> is rational and 
                    <m>(\sqrt{2}-x)</m> is rational. Since rationals are closed under addition
                    <md>
                      (\sqrt{2}+x)+(\sqrt{2}-x) = 2\sqrt{2}
                    </md>
                    is also rational. Since rationals are closed under multiplication, and <m>2\sqrt{2}</m> and <m>\frac{1}{2}</m>
                    are rational then 
                    <md>
                      \frac{1}{2}(2\sqrt{2}) = \sqrt{2}
                    </md>
                    is rational. This is a contradiction as we know <m>\sqrt{2}</m> is irrational. Thus, the negation of 
                    the theorem is false and the theorem is true. That is, for each real number
                     <m>x</m>, <m>(\sqrt{2}+x)</m> is irrational or <m>(\sqrt{2}-x)</m> is irrational.
    </p>
  </proof>

      </page>


      <page>
        <theorem >
          <statement>
            <p>
              For each real number <m>x</m>, if <m>x</m> is irrational, then <m>\sqrt[3]{x}</m> is irrational.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </theorem>
        
        <proof>
          <p>
             We will use a proof by contradiction.
                    So we assume the theorem is false.
                    That is, we assume that there exists a real number <m>x</m> such that <m>x</m> is irrational and 
                    <m>\sqrt[3]{x}</m> is rational. Since <m>\sqrt[3]{x}</m> is rational, there exist <m>m</m> and <m>n</m>
                    such that <m>\sqrt[3]{x} = \frac{m}{n}</m>. Cubing both sides, <m>x = \frac{m^3}{n^3}</m> and so <m>x</m>
                    is rational, which contradicts our assumption that <m>x</m> is rational.
          </p>
        </proof>

        <remark>
          <p>
            Note this proof could also be done by contrapositive. In general, if you start by assuming the conclusion
            is false (because of the negation) and you end up contradicting the hypothesis, you were just doing a proof 
            by contrapositive. I sometimes call this <q>contradiction-itis</q>. Once one learns proof by contradiction,
            one sometimes overuses it when other proof techniques would be clearer.
          </p>
        </remark>
      </page>

      <page>
        <theorem>
          <statement>
            <p>
              For each <m>a,b\in\mathbb{R}</m>, if <m>a\neq 0</m> and <m>b\neq 0</m> then <m>\sqrt{a^2+b^2} \neq a+b</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </theorem>

        <proof>
          <p>
            We will use a proof by contradiction.
                    So we assume the theorem is false.
                    That is, we assume that there exists <m>a,b\in\mathbb{R}</m> such that <m>a\neq 0</m> and <m>b\neq 0</m>
                    and <m>\sqrt{a^2+b^2} = a+b</m>. Then
                    <md>
                      <mrow>\sqrt{a^2+b^2} \amp = a+b </mrow>
                      <mrow>a^2 + b^2 \amp = (a+b)^2 </mrow>
                      <mrow>a^2+b^2 \amp = a^2+2ab+ b^2 </mrow>
                      <mrow> 0 = 2ab. \amp </mrow>
                    </md>
                However, since <m>a\neq 0</m> and <m>b\neq 0</m>, <m>2ab \neq 0</m>. So we have reached a 
                contradiction and the negation is false. Thus, for each <m>a,b\in\mathbb{R}</m>, 
                if <m>a\neq 0</m> and <m>b\neq 0</m> then <m>\sqrt{a^2+b^2} \neq a+b</m>.
          </p>
        </proof>
        
      </page>

      <page>
        <theorem>
          <statement>
            <p>
              For all integers <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>, if <m>5\nmid ab</m>, then <m>5\nmid a</m> and <m>5\nmid b</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </theorem>

        <proof>
          <p>
            We will use a proof by contradiction. So we assume the theorem is false, or that there exist integers <m>a</m>
            and <m>b</m> such that <m>5\nmid ab</m> and <m>5\mid a</m> or <m>5\mid b</m>. Without loss of generality, assume
            <m>5\mid a</m>. By definition of divides, there exists an integer <m>k</m> such that <m>5k= a</m>. Then
            <m>5kb=ab</m>. By closure, <m>kb\in \mathbb{Z}</m> so <m>5\mid ab</m>. However, we assumed <m>5\nmid ab</m>,
            so we've reached a contradiction. Thus, the negation of the theorem is false and for all integers <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>, 
            if <m>5\nmid ab</m>, then <m>5\nmid a</m> and <m>5\nmid b</m>.
          </p>
        </proof>
        
      </page>

      <page>
        <theorem>
          <statement>
            <p>
              For all integers <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>, if <m>5\mid ab</m> then <m>5\mid a</m> or <m>5\mid b</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </theorem>
        
        <proof>
          <p>
            We will use proof by contradiction so we assume the theorem is false. That is, suppose that
            there exist integers <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> such that <m>5\mid ab</m> and <m>5\nmid a</m> and <m>5\nmid b</m>.
            Since <m>5\nmid a</m> we know there exists <m>q,r \in \mathbb{Z}</m> with <m>0&lt; r &lt; 5</m> 
            such that <m>a = 5q+r</m>. Similarly, since <m>5 \nmid b</m>, there exist <m>q,r\in\mathbb{Z}</m> with <m>0 \lt; r' &lt; 5</m>
            such that <m>b = 5q' + r'</m>. By substitution and algebra,
              <md>
                <mrow>ab \amp = (5q+r)(5q'+r') </mrow>
                <mrow> \amp = 25qq' + 5qr' + 5q'r + rr' </mrow>
                <mrow> \amp = 5(5qq'+qr'+q'r) + rr'. </mrow>
              </md>
            Now, consider the possibilites for <m>r</m> and <m>r'</m>. There is no ordered pair <m>(r,r')\in \{1,2,3,4\}\times 
              \{1,2,3,4\} 
            </m> where <m>5 \mid rr'</m>. So <m>ab</m> has a nonzero remainder when divided by <m>5</m>.
            This is a contradiction, since we assumed <m>5\mid ab</m>. Thus the negation is false and for 
            all integers <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>, if <m>5\mid ab</m> then <m>5\mid a</m> or <m>5\mid b</m>.
          </p>
        </proof>

        <remark>
          <p>
            Another case of contradictionitis! Can you use the contrapositive here.
          </p>
        </remark>
      </page>
</handout>